1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,931
Twenty years. Sixty-eight days.

2
00:00:03,071 --> 00:00:06,796
No one had ever become Formula One world champion so young.

3
00:00:06,936 --> 00:00:09,563
But before the crown, there was everything else.

4
00:00:09,703 --> 00:00:14,936
A technical revolution. Two races cancelled. A war inside
the same garage.

5
00:00:15,076 --> 00:00:21,271
Lewis Hamilton back on the top step of the podium. A Max
Verstappen who refused to fade away.

6
00:00:21,411 --> 00:00:25,472
And a champion McLaren that suddenly had to start again from
zero.

7
00:00:25,612 --> 00:00:29,203
Nineteen Grands Prix. From Australia to Brazil.

8
00:00:29,343 --> 00:00:31,269
This is everything that happened.

9
00:00:39,909 --> 00:00:44,611
To understand 2026, you have to start before the first
start.

10
00:00:47,051 --> 00:00:51,316
This was the biggest rule change in Formula One's recent
history.

11
00:00:51,456 --> 00:00:58,960
Power units now split their output almost fifty-fifty: half
from the combustion engine, half from the electrical side.

12
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:07,749
The MGU-H was gone. In return, the MGU-K almost tripled,
from one hundred and twenty to three hundred and fifty
kilowatts.

13
00:01:07,889 --> 00:01:10,919
Every car switched to advanced sustainable fuel.

14
00:01:11,059 --> 00:01:19,087
The cars became shorter, narrower and lighter: the minimum
weight dropped to seven hundred and sixty-eight kilograms,
without fuel.

15
00:01:19,227 --> 00:01:26,835
And DRS was gone. In its place came active aerodynamics:
front and rear wings that change angle during the lap.

16
00:01:26,975 --> 00:01:34,951
To overtake, drivers got an overtake mode with extra energy,
plus a boost button to deploy the battery whenever they
wanted.

17
00:01:35,091 --> 00:01:41,518
The result: managing energy became as important as braking
late. And the racing changed character.

18
00:01:42,858 --> 00:01:47,845
The grid changed too. Sauber became Audi, with Hulkenberg
and Bortoleto.

19
00:01:47,985 --> 00:01:54,258
Cadillac arrived as the eleventh team, with two veterans:
Sergio Perez and Valtteri Bottas.

20
00:01:54,398 --> 00:02:02,384
Isack Hadjar was promoted to Red Bull alongside Verstappen,
and Arvid Lindblad was the only rookie, at Racing Bulls.

21
00:02:02,524 --> 00:02:07,805
Twenty-two cars. Eleven teams. And Lando Norris defending
the world title.

22
00:02:10,045 --> 00:02:14,029
Before the season even started, the first controversy broke
out.

23
00:02:14,169 --> 00:02:25,383
Ferrari, Audi and Honda suspected Mercedes had found a way,
with a hot engine, to exceed the sixteen-to-one maximum
compression ratio, which was only measured cold.

24
00:02:25,523 --> 00:02:32,301
Mercedes always insisted the engine was legal, and that it
had consulted the FIA throughout development.

25
00:02:32,441 --> 00:02:38,014
The FIA changed the rule: from the first of June,
compression would also be measured hot.

26
00:02:38,154 --> 00:02:46,504
Rivals put the gain at around thirteen horsepower. Mercedes
disputed those numbers, and Toto Wolff called it a storm in
a teacup.

27
00:02:48,544 --> 00:02:51,862
Melbourne. The eighth of March. The new era begins.

28
00:02:58,302 --> 00:03:05,929
The drama began before the start. Oscar Piastri, at his home
race, crashed on the way to the grid and never took the
start.

29
00:03:06,069 --> 00:03:11,988
Nico Hulkenberg was also withdrawn just before the race.
Only twenty cars started.

30
00:03:12,128 --> 00:03:17,356
Ferrari got off the line brilliantly, and Charles Leclerc
was in the fight at the front.

31
00:03:17,496 --> 00:03:27,525
But the decisive moment came under the virtual safety car.
Mercedes double-stacked both cars; Ferrari didn't make the
same call and lost control of the race.

32
00:03:27,665 --> 00:03:36,727
George Russell won, with Kimi Antonelli less than three
seconds behind. A Mercedes one-two in the very first race,
with Leclerc third.

33
00:03:36,867 --> 00:03:41,855
Hamilton was fourth, Norris fifth. And rookie Lindblad
scored on debut, in eighth.

34
00:03:51,295 --> 00:03:59,715
In Shanghai, the first bombshell: neither McLaren started.
Two different electrical problems, both linked to the
Mercedes power unit.

35
00:03:59,855 --> 00:04:03,007
With two more cars out, only eighteen took the start.

36
00:04:03,147 --> 00:04:07,955
Russell had already won Saturday's sprint. But Sunday
belonged to Antonelli.

37
00:04:08,095 --> 00:04:14,673
He started from pole, lost the lead to Hamilton at the
start, took it back, and controlled the race to the flag.

38
00:04:14,813 --> 00:04:19,219
His first career win, at nineteen. Only Verstappen had won
younger.

39
00:04:19,359 --> 00:04:22,947
And Hamilton, at last, stood on the podium for Ferrari.

40
00:04:28,887 --> 00:04:38,140
Days later came the news: because of the conflict in the
Middle East, the Bahrain and Saudi Arabian Grands Prix were
cancelled, with no replacements.

41
00:04:38,280 --> 00:04:43,815
The calendar shrank to twenty-two races, with a five-week
gap between Japan and Miami.

42
00:04:49,155 --> 00:04:54,377
At Suzuka, Antonelli took pole again, but made a poor start
and dropped to sixth.

43
00:04:54,517 --> 00:05:02,123
The race turned on Oliver Bearman's crash at Spoon. The
safety car shuffled the order, and Antonelli was back in
front.

44
00:05:02,263 --> 00:05:09,491
He won by thirteen seconds from Piastri, who finally
finished a race and took second. Leclerc completed the
podium.

45
00:05:09,631 --> 00:05:16,216
Antonelli became the youngest ever multiple Grand Prix
winner, and the youngest championship leader in history.

46
00:05:25,656 --> 00:05:33,169
After five weeks, Miami. On Saturday, Norris won the sprint:
McLaren's first victory of 2026.

47
00:05:33,309 --> 00:05:39,895
Hadjar was disqualified from qualifying: his Red Bull's
floor was two millimetres outside the rules.

48
00:05:40,035 --> 00:05:45,134
The threat of storms moved the start forward by three hours,
but the rain barely came.

49
00:05:45,274 --> 00:05:53,052
Leclerc led early but spun. He lost fourth to Russell and,
on the line, fifth to Verstappen. Both were later penalised.

50
00:05:53,192 --> 00:06:00,962
Up front, Antonelli held off Norris by just over three
seconds. Three wins in a row, and a twenty-point lead over
Russell.

51
00:06:10,402 --> 00:06:13,883
In Montreal, the civil war at Mercedes exploded.

52
00:06:14,023 --> 00:06:23,244
In the sprint, Russell and Antonelli made contact. Antonelli
was forced onto the grass and demanded, over the radio, a
penalty for his team-mate.

53
00:06:23,384 --> 00:06:28,673
Toto Wolff told him to concentrate on the driving, not on
moaning over the radio.

54
00:06:28,813 --> 00:06:35,153
On Sunday they fought wheel to wheel again, until Russell
retired from the lead with a power unit problem.

55
00:06:35,293 --> 00:06:39,253
Norris retired too, after a tyre gamble that backfired.

56
00:06:39,393 --> 00:06:48,194
Antonelli won a fourth in a row. Hamilton was second, his
best Ferrari result yet, and Verstappen took his first
podium of the year.

57
00:06:48,334 --> 00:06:55,622
The lead grew to forty-three points. And Mercedes drew up
new internal rules on how its drivers could race each other.

58
00:07:05,062 --> 00:07:11,321
In Monaco, the first race under the new compression test,
Verstappen was out on the very first lap.

59
00:07:11,461 --> 00:07:21,380
Lance Stroll crashed at the final corner and brought out the
safety car. At the restart, Leclerc crashed in the same
place, at his home race, and blamed his brakes.

60
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:27,675
Then the track surface began breaking up at that corner, and
the race was red-flagged with ten laps to go.

61
00:07:27,815 --> 00:07:33,546
Antonelli led every lap and survived the restart. Hamilton
second, Hadjar third.

62
00:07:33,686 --> 00:07:41,908
A fifth straight win. The youngest Monaco winner ever, and
Mercedes' first victory in the Principality since 2019.

63
00:07:51,348 --> 00:07:53,743
Barcelona. And finally, it happened.

64
00:07:53,883 --> 00:07:58,804
Ferrari went for three stops and nailed the timing of the
virtual safety car.

65
00:07:58,944 --> 00:08:04,904
Antonelli retired late on while fighting for second.
Mercedes' unbeaten run was over.

66
00:08:05,044 --> 00:08:14,352
And Lewis Hamilton won. His first victory with Ferrari, his
first since Belgium in 2024, and the one hundred and sixth
of his career.

67
00:08:14,492 --> 00:08:20,609
Russell was second, Norris third, Verstappen fourth. And
Alpine got both cars into the points.

68
00:08:30,049 --> 00:08:36,811
In Austria, Russell hit back. Pole position and victory,
with Verstappen just over a second and a half behind.

69
00:08:36,951 --> 00:08:42,024
But the story of the day was something else: Verstappen
against Hamilton, once again.

70
00:08:42,164 --> 00:08:49,876
During their fight, Verstappen said on the radio it was a
clear penalty. The stewards looked at it and took no further
action.

71
00:08:50,016 --> 00:08:55,949
In Hamilton's view, it was Verstappen who should have backed
out. Opposing opinions, no punishment.

72
00:08:56,089 --> 00:09:02,301
Hamilton was fifth, Leclerc only eighth. At Red Bull's home
race, Ferrari had a day to forget.

73
00:09:11,741 --> 00:09:16,666
At Silverstone, Antonelli won the sprint. But Sunday
belonged to Ferrari.

74
00:09:16,806 --> 00:09:25,024
Charles Leclerc won for the first time since 2024. It was
the two hundred and fiftieth victory in Ferrari's history.

75
00:09:25,164 --> 00:09:33,018
But the finish was tarnished. After a late crash for
Verstappen, a message appeared saying the safety car would
come in that lap.

76
00:09:33,158 --> 00:09:42,096
The FIA overruled it eight seconds later and blamed a
software error. The race finished behind the safety car, to
boos from the grandstands.

77
00:09:42,236 --> 00:09:51,318
Russell was less than half a second behind. And Antonelli,
hit by reliability trouble, finished sixteenth. The lead
fell to twenty-five points.

78
00:10:00,758 --> 00:10:07,297
Spa. Lap one. Russell goes around the outside, touches
Hamilton and ends up in the gravel. Race over.

79
00:10:07,437 --> 00:10:14,428
The stewards held Hamilton wholly responsible, but cut the
penalty to five seconds because it was the opening lap.

80
00:10:14,568 --> 00:10:21,808
Hamilton and Ferrari team boss Fred Vasseur disagreed with
the decision. Hamilton said he felt unlucky.

81
00:10:21,948 --> 00:10:28,254
There was also an unsafe release in the pits, in which a
mechanic was knocked over. Ferrari were fined.

82
00:10:28,394 --> 00:10:35,252
Antonelli retook the lead after a restart and beat Leclerc
by under two seconds. His sixth win of the year.

83
00:10:44,692 --> 00:10:47,242
Before the summer break, McLaren woke up.

84
00:10:47,382 --> 00:10:56,590
Norris started from pole. Piastri collided with Carlos
Sainz's Williams at turn two, which opened the door for
Norris in the pit strategy.

85
00:10:56,730 --> 00:10:59,932
Piastri would later retire with a gearbox failure.

86
00:11:00,072 --> 00:11:06,001
Norris won for the first time in 2026, fifteen seconds clear
of Verstappen.

87
00:11:06,141 --> 00:11:12,444
Antonelli was third and extended his lead. Hamilton lost a
place to a five-second penalty.

88
00:11:17,884 --> 00:11:26,738
At the summer break, the picture was clear: Mercedes
dominated, Ferrari won now and then, and McLaren and Red
Bull were searching for answers.

89
00:11:26,878 --> 00:11:33,557
At the back, Honda-powered Aston Martin reached the break
with a single point, scored by Alonso in Monaco.

90
00:11:33,697 --> 00:11:38,384
And Cadillac had yet to score, with nine retirements in
eleven races.

91
00:11:43,724 --> 00:11:48,165
Zandvoort hosted the final Dutch Grand Prix. Russell won the
sprint.

92
00:11:48,305 --> 00:11:56,541
On Sunday, the banked final corner was wet. Verstappen, at
home, lost the car at the end of the opening lap and crashed
heavily.

93
00:11:56,681 --> 00:12:01,343
He walked away and admitted he had been too aggressive on
the throttle. Red flag.

94
00:12:01,483 --> 00:12:08,082
At the restart, Norris passed Antonelli and Hamilton on the
same lap and won by eleven and a half seconds.

95
00:12:08,222 --> 00:12:12,806
Antonelli second, Russell third. The lead grew to fifty-nine
points.

96
00:12:22,246 --> 00:12:27,883
Monza. The nineteen-year-old Italian started nineteenth,
after a power unit penalty.

97
00:12:28,023 --> 00:12:32,940
Leclerc, Ferrari's great hope, crashed at the Parabolica on
the very first lap.

98
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:39,409
And Antonelli climbed, car by car, until he passed Russell
on the run to Ascari and took the lead.

99
00:12:39,549 --> 00:12:48,102
He won by almost four seconds. The first Italian to win at
Monza since Ludovico Scarfiotti, in 1966.

100
00:12:48,242 --> 00:12:57,104
And the second-lowest grid position for a winner in Formula
One history, behind only John Watson at Long Beach in 1983.

101
00:13:06,544 --> 00:13:12,114
Madrid hosted Formula One for the first time in forty-five
years, on a brand-new circuit.

102
00:13:12,254 --> 00:13:21,816
Norris started from pole and was leading. But the virtual
safety car came two seconds too late for him, and Antonelli
pitted at exactly the right moment.

103
00:13:21,956 --> 00:13:28,958
Antonelli won, Verstappen was second, Norris third. Hamilton
retired on lap seven with a brake failure.

104
00:13:29,098 --> 00:13:35,435
Many critics called it a forgettable race. Not for the
championship: the lead reached eighty-one points.

105
00:13:44,875 --> 00:13:48,846
In Baku, Russell started from pole and led from lights to
flag.

106
00:13:48,986 --> 00:13:54,436
Two safety car periods gave Verstappen the chance to attack
right to the final corner.

107
00:13:54,576 --> 00:14:01,508
Russell won by one hundred and ninety-six thousandths of a
second. Hadjar completed the podium for Red Bull.

108
00:14:01,648 --> 00:14:06,765
Six drivers retired, including both McLarens, who left Baku
without a point.

109
00:14:06,905 --> 00:14:13,170
Antonelli, after starting in the midfield, recovered to
fifth. The lead fell to sixty-six points.

110
00:14:22,610 --> 00:14:25,720
In Singapore, Verstappen had the perfect weekend.

111
00:14:25,860 --> 00:14:29,690
He won the sprint, a wet race in which Russell crashed while
leading.

112
00:14:29,830 --> 00:14:34,866
On Sunday he took pole and led every lap. His first ever win
in Singapore.

113
00:14:35,006 --> 00:14:42,771
Hamilton stayed out on old tyres during a virtual safety
car, dropped from third to sixth, and apologised to his
team.

114
00:14:42,911 --> 00:14:48,145
Leclerc was second, Antonelli third. The title was getting
closer and closer.

115
00:14:57,585 --> 00:15:03,407
In Austin, Antonelli delivered what Toto Wolff called the
best race of his career so far.

116
00:15:03,547 --> 00:15:10,885
A single stop, at the end of lap twenty-four, and total
control. Leclerc thirteen seconds back, Verstappen third.

117
00:15:11,025 --> 00:15:15,358
Hamilton was fourth, Norris fifth. The title was one step
away.

118
00:15:24,798 --> 00:15:31,841
Mexico City. Antonelli arrived one hundred and twenty-three
points clear of Russell. Ninth place would be enough.

119
00:15:31,981 --> 00:15:38,414
The race was chaos. Valtteri Bottas had a heavy crash at
turn one, and the race was red-flagged.

120
00:15:38,554 --> 00:15:46,964
Then came a standing restart, more safety cars, and a
collision between Piastri and Lindblad that earned Piastri a
ten-second penalty.

121
00:15:47,104 --> 00:15:51,055
Both Alpines were also penalised for ignoring yellow flags.

122
00:15:51,195 --> 00:15:57,783
Verstappen beat Hamilton by eight tenths. And Antonelli, in
third, crossed the line as world champion.

123
00:16:04,023 --> 00:16:07,484
Kimi Antonelli. Twenty years and sixty-eight days.

124
00:16:07,624 --> 00:16:12,766
The youngest champion in history, younger than Sebastian
Vettel in 2010.

125
00:16:12,906 --> 00:16:18,034
The first Italian champion since Alberto Ascari, in 1953.

126
00:16:18,174 --> 00:16:27,655
And Mercedes' first drivers' title since Hamilton in 2020.
Won in only his second Formula One season, with four races
to spare.

127
00:16:32,695 --> 00:16:36,882
The champion was decided. But Brazil still had a story to
tell.

128
00:16:43,322 --> 00:16:47,306
Saturday. Rain delayed qualifying by almost an hour and a
half.

129
00:16:47,446 --> 00:16:57,379
When it finally ran, Norris was the king of the wet: pole
position, his third in a row at Interlagos, with Russell two
tenths behind and Antonelli third.

130
00:16:57,519 --> 00:17:08,032
Hamilton and Verstappen were knocked out in Q2 and also
carried engine-change penalties. Both Ferraris took ten-
place drops for fitting a fifth combustion engine.

131
00:17:08,172 --> 00:17:11,657
Hamilton started twentieth. Verstappen, twenty-first.

132
00:17:11,797 --> 00:17:18,035
Sunday. Seventy-one laps. The track started damp, dried out,
and then the rain returned at the end.

133
00:17:18,175 --> 00:17:24,919
Norris managed everything from the front, without a mistake,
on a day with no safety car and no crashes.

134
00:17:25,059 --> 00:17:31,831
And from the back came Verstappen. Car by car, pass after
pass, from twenty-first all the way to second.

135
00:17:31,971 --> 00:17:41,901
Hadjar completed the podium for Red Bull, twenty-four
seconds back. Russell was fourth and Antonelli fifth;
according to reports, he let his team-mate through.

136
00:17:42,041 --> 00:17:49,386
Hamilton, in his four hundredth Grand Prix, recovered from
twentieth to ninth despite a spin, but finished a lap down.

137
00:17:49,526 --> 00:17:53,987
Norris won by fourteen and a half seconds, his third victory
of the year.

138
00:17:54,127 --> 00:18:00,966
And with points from Russell and Antonelli, Mercedes
clinched the constructors' title. The ninth in their
history.

139
00:18:01,106 --> 00:18:07,178
Verstappen stretched his advantage in second place in the
championship from two points to seventeen.

140
00:18:13,318 --> 00:18:16,520
Nineteen races in, where does every team stand?

141
00:18:19,060 --> 00:18:29,843
Mercedes: the best reading of the new rules. Twelve wins
from nineteen Grands Prix and both titles secured, despite
reliability failures and tension between the drivers.

142
00:18:29,983 --> 00:18:40,918
Ferrari: second among the teams, with wins for Hamilton in
Barcelona and Leclerc at Silverstone. But strategy errors,
from Australia to Singapore, proved costly.

143
00:18:41,058 --> 00:18:51,354
Red Bull: without dominating, Verstappen won in Singapore
and Mexico and was the most consistent force of the year.
Hadjar reached the podium three times.

144
00:18:51,494 --> 00:19:02,606
McLaren: the world champions began with two races without
Piastri and a double no-show in China. Norris has won three
times, but the team is still fighting for third.

145
00:19:02,746 --> 00:19:08,734
Racing Bulls are best of the rest, with more than one
hundred points. Lindblad scored on debut.

146
00:19:08,874 --> 00:19:15,045
Alpine, now with Mercedes power, are sixth, with Gasly and
Colapinto scoring regularly.

147
00:19:15,185 --> 00:19:20,047
Haas and Audi are fighting for seventh. Williams are having
a difficult year.

148
00:19:20,187 --> 00:19:28,779
Aston Martin, with Honda, have had a very hard season, with
only seven points. And newcomers Cadillac are still
searching for their first point.

149
00:19:30,819 --> 00:19:37,729
The eleventh of November, 2026. Three races to go: Las
Vegas, Qatar and Abu Dhabi.

150
00:19:40,669 --> 00:19:44,182
Antonelli is champion with three hundred and ninety points.

151
00:19:44,322 --> 00:19:54,500
But the fight for runner-up is wide open. Verstappen has two
hundred and seventy-nine. Russell, two hundred and sixty-
two. Hamilton, just one point behind.

152
00:19:54,640 --> 00:19:59,429
Leclerc has two hundred and forty-nine, and Norris two
hundred and forty-six.

153
00:19:59,569 --> 00:20:07,145
With seventy-five points still available, these five
drivers, separated by thirty-three points, can all still
finish second.

154
00:20:10,985 --> 00:20:20,823
In the constructors' championship, Mercedes are already
champions, with six hundred and fifty-two points. Ferrari
are second, with more than five hundred.

155
00:20:20,963 --> 00:20:27,470
The big battle is for third: Red Bull on four hundred and
fifteen, McLaren seventeen points behind.

156
00:20:27,610 --> 00:20:32,190
And at the back, Aston Martin and Cadillac are fighting to
avoid last place.

157
00:20:35,430 --> 00:20:38,983
A season of new rules, new faces and old rivalries.

158
00:20:39,123 --> 00:20:45,865
A twenty-year-old champion. A seven-time champion who won
again. A Verstappen who never stopped fighting.

159
00:20:46,005 --> 00:20:51,255
The title has its owner. But there are still three races
left to write the final chapter.

160
00:20:51,395 --> 00:20:55,697
This was Formula One in 2026. Up to Brazil.

